speechtext:
I said, if the order had been included in the Statute of 1914, in section 6, it would necessarily expire at the termination of the war. My hon. friend dealt with the matter this afternoon as if the War Measures Act were a permanent measure. I stated that there are some clauses in it which are of a permanent

character and the effect of which may be spoken of as perpetual, whereas there are other clauses- and the most important clauses so far as the present question is concerned- which are of a temporary character. I would call the attention of hon. members to the War Measures Act, from which they will see that I am correct in that statement. This Act was passed on the 27th of August, 1914. First is the citation:
This Act may be cited as The War Measures Act, 1914.
Then by section 2, it provides:
All Acts and things done or omitted to be done prior to the passing of this Act and on or after the first day of August, A.D. 1914, by or under the authority of or ratified1 by,-
(a)	His Majesty the King in Council;
(b)	any Minister or officer of His Majesty's Imperial Government;
(c)	the Governor in Council;
(d)	any Minister or officer of the Government of Canada;
(e)	any other authority or person;
which, had they been done or omitted after the passing of this Act, would have been authorised by this Act or by orders or regulations hereunder, shall be deemed to have been done or omitted under the authority of this Act, and are hereby declared to have been lawfully done or omitted.
That, of course, has no application to the present question. Then it is provided by section 3:
The provisions of sections 6, 10, 11 and 13 of this Act shall only be in force during war, invasion, or insurrection, real or apprehended.
Sections 4, 5, 7, 8, 9 and 12 are in the nature of perpetual legislation.
Now, it is under the 6th section, which is declared to 'be temporary and to remain in force only during the war, that this Order in Council was passed. Section 6 says:-
6. The Governor in Council shall have power to do and authorize such acts and things, and to make from time to time such orders and regulations as he may by reason of the existence of real or apprehended war, invasion or insurrection deem necessary or advisable for the security, defence, peace, order and welfare of Canada; and for greater certainty, but not so as to restrict the generality of the foregoing terms, it is hereby declared that the powers of the Governor in Council shall extend to all matters coming within the classes of subjects hereinafter enumerated, that is to say:-[DOT]
(a)	Censorship and the control and suppres-' sion of publications, writings, maps, plans,
photographs, communications and means of communication;
(b)	arrest, detention, exclusion and deportation ;
(c)	control of the harbours, ports and territorial waters of Canada and the movements of vessels;

(cl) transportation by land, air, or water, and the control of the transport of persons and things;
Next is the clause under which this Order was passed.
(e)	trading, exportation, importation, production and manufacture;
(f)	appropriation, control, forfeiture and disposition of property and of the use thereof.
To make my argument at this point clearer, let me ask, if this Order in Council is to have the same effect as a statute what would have been the effect if, instead of paragraph (e), which gives authority to make Orders in Council relating to trading, exportation, importation, production and manufacture, the Act had declared that wheat, wheat products and semolina should be placed on the free list? I admit that the Order jn Council is just as strong as would have been a clause to that effect in the Act, but in that case it would, of course, come under section 6 and, toy section 3, section 6 is to be in force only during the war. Therefore, unquestionably, if, instead of the Order in Council passed by the Governor in Council under the authority of section 6, Parliament had exercised this authority itself. its effect would cease at the end of the war. Now, the question is, does the Order in Council have a more extensive operation than the section of the statute on which it is founded? I submit not. It is merely subordinate legislation which Parliament, instead of enacting itself, has given authority to the Governor in Council to enact. As I have said, it is similar to the case of 'boards of health established by provincial legislatures and given power to make regulations. If the Act establishing the board of 'health is temporary in its character the moment the time arrives when the Act 'becomes inoperative then ipso facto any orders or regulations made by the hoard of health cease to have any effect. If there can be found in the statute itself any evidence of an intention on the part of Parliament that any Act which was done by Order in Council should remain in operation after the Act itself went out of force, then a court would construe it as being the intention of Parliament that it should be so continued in operation after the Act had terminated, but under the authorities there must be a clear intention on the part of Parliament that anything which is done shall continue in force after the Act has ceased to operate. My, hon. friend the Solicitor General said, " If the suggestion of hon. gentlemen opposite is correct, how would it be with regard
to the Ross Rifle factory? The Government has expropriated that property; suppose they should not pay for the property until after the war ceases, would the property revert back to the Ross Rifle Company?" Well, Parliament had that in view, and by section 7. which is of the nature of perpetual legislation, provides that the Government shall pay for any property7 that has been expropriated. Parliament realized that if all the sections of the Act were to be of the same temporary character as sections
6,	10. II and 13 there might 'be' cases where grave injustice would toe done, and section
7,	therefore, provides:-
Whenever any property or the use thereof has been appropriated by His Majesty under the provisions of this Act, or any order in council, order or regulation made thereunder, and compensation is to be made therefor and has not been agreed upon, the claim shall be referred by the Minister of Justice to the Exohequer Court, or to a Superior or County Court of the province within which the claim arises, or to a judge of any such court.
That section does not end with the war, as do sections 6, 10, 11 and 13. Section 7 provides for the payment of any property which has been expropriated under the temporary Act.
